{"format":"local-vault-ai-portable-chunks-v1","version":1,"chunks":[{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Probability Distribution.md::0","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Probability Distribution.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"probability distribution probability distribution","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Probability Distribution.md","fileName":"Probability Distribution.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Probability Distribution","heading":"Document","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"2026-09-05 14:36\n\nStatus: #baby\n\nTags: [[Game Analytics and Probability]] [[Game Balance and Progression]] [[Probability Foundations]]","tags":["baby"],"links":["Game Analytics and Probability","Game Balance and Progression","Probability Foundations","advancedgamedesign.pdf","bayesianprogramming.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Probability Distribution.md::1","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Probability Distribution.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"probability distribution probability distribution","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Probability Distribution.md","fileName":"Probability Distribution.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Probability Distribution","heading":"Probability Distribution","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"A probability distribution assigns a likelihood to each possible outcome in a range. A uniform distribution gives outcomes equal chances, while combining independent events can create a nonuniform shape such as the bell-like distribution of two dice.\n\nThe distribution's mean, mode, median, and range describe different aspects of its shape. Choosing a distribution changes the character of uncertainty, so an attribute value has different meaning under a flat range than under a clustered one.\n\nIn Bayesian reasoning, a probability distribution assigns normalized plausibility across the alternatives of a variable. Joint distributions describe several variables together, conditional distributions incorporate known information, and marginal distributions remove variables that are not part of the question.","tags":["baby"],"links":["Game Analytics and Probability","Game Balance and Progression","Probability Foundations","advancedgamedesign.pdf","bayesianprogramming.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Probability Distribution.md::2","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Probability Distribution.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"probability distribution probability distribution","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Probability Distribution.md","fileName":"Probability Distribution.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Probability Distribution","heading":"References","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"[[advancedgamedesign.pdf]]\n\n[[bayesianprogramming.pdf]]","tags":["baby"],"links":["Game Analytics and Probability","Game Balance and Progression","Probability Foundations","advancedgamedesign.pdf","bayesianprogramming.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""}]}